# NORTHSTAR — Architecture North Star, Language Grammar, AST & Type System Specification
File: openOODA/NORTHSTAR.oot

This document defines the target architecture, formal grammar, AST design, and type system.
Verified release state: openOODA governance v1.0.7 / openOODA/std v1.0.3 / openOODA/oodar v2.0.0 / openOODA/ooda v2.11.x.

openOODA is a systems programming language engineered for safe, high-performance, deterministic software.

Note: per the 2026-09-02 governance refactor, the 13 canonical process boards have been reduced to 3 onboarding docs (ANCHOR.oot, this file, RULES.oot) + 6 RFCs + 2 practice files. See openOODA/ANCHOR.oo for the entry point.
The compiler verifies source code ahead-of-time.
All side effects and privileged operations require explicit, unforgeable capability tokens.
Functions declare verifiable contracts (`requires`, `ensures`, `spec`).
The compiler produces machine-readable diagnostics with automated one-turn repairs.

## 1. Architectural Foundation & The 10 Strategic Governance Pillars

### 1.1 Core Principles & Informational E-M
- Pillar 1 (First Principles & Pure File Extension Laws): Pure .oo and .oot files only. Derive algorithms and grammar directly from hardware specs, IEEE 754, NIST KATs, and IETF RFCs.
- Pillar 2 (Boyd's E-M Invariant & Zero Drag): Calculate performance as E-M = ((T - D) / W) * V. Strict <= 256 lines per file. Maximize instruction throughput (T). Minimize drag (D) via linear arenas and O(1) resets. Zero intermediate artifacts in std/.
- Pillar 3 (Adversarial Validation & Double-Run Invariant): 8-Dimensional Red Team verification (e1..e8) and 8-Gate Blocking Veto Protocol. Double-run tests: Run_1(Test) == Run_2(Test).
- Pillar 4 (Academy 4-Element Headers): ASD-STE100 headers (# Title, Logline, Setup, Beats on .oo; Title, File on .oot (2-line clean header; version SSoT in ooda.pkg)).
- Pillar 5 (Capability Security & Zero Ambient Authority): 14 explicit unforgeable capability tokens (&FsReadCap, &FsWriteCap, &ProcessCap, etc.). Compile-time SMT check elision and self-hosted bootstrapping.
- Pillar 6 (Multi-Agent Swarm Safety & Topology Invariance): Stationary workspace topology (Delta_path = 0). Fail-closed agent fork bomb rejection, AST reflection (`ooda outline`), automated repair (`ooda fix`), and collaborative swarm protocols.
- Pillar 7 (Formal RFC Template Compliance): All RFCs in openOODA/rfcs/ follow 0000-template.oot with mandatory Security and Testing sections.
- Pillar 8 (Two-Binary Architecture): Decoupled compiler (oodac) and product CLI driver (ooda).
- Pillar 9 (Gen 1 C/H Substrate Isolation): Low-level C shims (oodar.c, oodar.h, types.h) strictly confined to the oodar/ repo. Zero C header leaks into openOODA/std/. The oodar/ repo is the canonical home of the Gen 1 host substrate; its public ABI is the set of oo_* symbols exported by the 6 per-domain subdirs (core, sec, fs, net, hw, app) per the v2.0.0 6-subdir layout.
- Pillar 10 (Zero Loose Unheaded Files & Clean Workspaces): Zero loose unheaded .oo files in roots, zero struct trailing commas, clean workspace.
- Subtractive Optimization: Improve software by removing unneeded runtime state and ambient privileges.
- Deterministic Execution: Produce bit-identical binaries with zero-timestamp digests.

### 1.2 Generational Architecture Hierarchy (Gen 1 to Gen 7)
Each tier maps to a fighter generation. Gen 8 is unknowable and intentionally undefined.
- Gen 1 (F-86 / MiG-15 — Silicon Substrate): Page tables (x86_64 PML4, RISC-V Sv39), SIMD registers, MMU, and APIC/GIC interrupt routing. Canonical home: `openOODA/oodar/` (the Gen 1 C host substrate; oo_* symbols across 6 per-domain subdirs per the v2.0.0 layout).
- Gen 2 (F-104 / MiG-21 — Self-Hosted Compiler & IR): Lexer, parser, Merkle AST, SSA mem2reg, optimization passes, and direct ELF64/WASM emission.
- Gen 3 (F-4 / MiG-23 — Deterministic Runtime & Memory): Linear arena allocators with O(1) bulk resets, deterministic ARC, and Landlock isolation.
- Gen 4 (F-14 / F-16 — Language Semantics & Type System): ADTs, 14 capability tokens, refinement types, typestates, SECRET taint tracking, and SMT contracts.
- Gen 5 (F-22 / F-35 — Distributed Agent Teams): Multi-agent coordination, sensor fusion mesh, P2P verification in O(log M), and automated one-turn repair.
- Gen 6 (NGAD / MUM-T — Autonomous Synthesis): Intent synthesis, real-time AST mutation, verifiable compute (ZK-STARK), and self-healing.
- Gen 7 (Quantum Swarm of Swarms): Autonomous 9-mote quantum swarm, continuous relativistic kinematics, and singularity cannon. Aspirational — not realizable today.
- Layer Encapsulation: Gen 1 through Gen 4 form a stable library layer for Gen 5 through Gen 7 agent teams.

### 1.3 Memory & Concurrency Model
- Linear arena allocators provide zero-heap standard library operations with bulk resets.
- Automatic Reference Counting (ARC) reclaims memory deterministically when reference counts reach zero.
- Buffer slicing operates with zero memory copies.
- Actor-based concurrency isolates memory heaps behind typed, bounded channels.
- Speculative execution uses transactional rollback buffers before commits.

### 1.4 Fundamental Operational Laws
- Law of Substratum Integrity: Fix base memory and concurrency drag before implementing high-level orchestration.
- Law of Hermetic Encapsulation: Treat each subsystem as an encapsulated module with explicit capability tokens.
- Law of Bounded Promotion: Require formal SMT proofs or invariant checks before runtime promotion of dynamic code.

### 1.5 Generational Semantic Versioning & 80/20 Floor Rule
- Version Syntax: `v<Floor>.<Thrust>.<Patch>`. openOODA governance repos release at v1.0.0; the ooda toolchain (compiler + runtime) advances on its own 2.11.x line.
- Floor (F): Increments on breaking changes to memory invariants, capability ABI, or bootstrap compiler.
- Thrust (T): Increments on performance improvements and non-breaking feature additions.
- Patch (P): Increments on bug repairs and security remediation.
- Power Law 80/20 Floor Rule: Increment Floor only when the 20% core foundational primitives change.

## 2. Language Grammar & Concrete Syntax

### 2.1 File & Module Organization
- Source files use `.oo`; documentation files use `.oot`.
- Each `.oo` file represents an isolated module.
- All files must contain a maximum of 256 lines.
- Product modules must begin with a 4-element Academy header (Title, Logline, Setup, Beats).

### 2.2 Core Syntax Rules
- Bindings are immutable by default (`let x = val;`); mutable bindings require `let mut x = val;`.
- Expressions yield values: blocks, `if/else`, and `match` expressions return results.
- The language contains no null pointers; use `Option[T]` and `Result[T, E]`.
- Handlers must explicitly process `Result` and `Option` returns using `match` or `?`.
- Functions declare contract clauses (`requires`, `ensures`, `spec`).

### 2.3 Formal EBNF Grammar
```
Program           ::= { Item } ;
Item              ::= FunctionDecl | TypeDecl | TraitDecl | ImportDecl ;
ImportDecl        ::= "import" Identifier [ "::" Identifier ] ";" ;
TypeDecl          ::= "type" Identifier "=" ( SumType | StructType | RefinementType | Newtype ) ";" ;
SumType           ::= Variant { "|" Variant } ;
Variant           ::= Identifier [ "(" Type ")" ] ;
StructType        ::= "struct" "{" { FieldDecl } "}" ;
FieldDecl         ::= Identifier ":" Type [ "," ] ;
RefinementType    ::= Type "where" Expression ;
Newtype           ::= "newtype" Type ;
FunctionDecl      ::= [ "pub" ] "fn" Identifier "(" [ ParameterList ] ")" [ "->" Type ]
                      [ ContractSpec ] Block [ VerifyBlock ] ;
ParameterList     ::= Parameter { "," Parameter } ;
Parameter         ::= Identifier ":" [ "&" ] Type ;
ContractSpec      ::= { Precondition | Postcondition | SpecClause } ;
Precondition      ::= "requires" Expression ;
Postcondition     ::= "ensures" Expression ;
SpecClause        ::= "spec" StringLiteral ;
VerifyBlock       ::= "verify" Identifier "{" { Statement } "}" ;
Statement         ::= LetStmt | ReturnStmt | ExprStmt | WhileStmt | AssignStmt ;
LetStmt           ::= "let" [ "mut" ] Identifier [ ":" Type ] "=" Expression ";" ;
ReturnStmt        ::= "return" [ Expression ] ";" ;
ExprStmt          ::= Expression ";" ;
WhileStmt         ::= "while" Expression Block ;
AssignStmt        ::= Identifier "=" Expression ";" ;
Expression        ::= IfExpr | MatchExpr | BinaryExpr | CallExpr | PrimaryExpr ;
IfExpr            ::= "if" Expression Block [ "else" ( Block | IfExpr ) ] ;
MatchExpr         ::= "match" Expression "{" { MatchArm } "}" ;
MatchArm          ::= Pattern "=>" ( Expression "," | Block ) ;
Pattern           ::= Identifier [ "(" Identifier ")" ] | Literal | "_" ;
CallExpr          ::= PrimaryExpr "(" [ ArgList ] ")" [ "?" ] ;
```

## 3. Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) Design

### 3.1 Tree Hierarchy & Strongly Typed Nodes
- Root `Program` node contains an ordered sequence of `Item` nodes.
- Strongly typed nodes represent statements, expressions, patterns, types, and contracts.
- Every AST node preserves byte-range source coordinates (file, line, column, length) for diagnostics.
- Function and call nodes maintain resolved capability signatures.

### 3.2 Pure Compile-Time Macro Expansion
- Compile-time macro functions evaluate during compilation.
- Macro functions ingest AST subtrees and emit rewritten AST nodes before code generation.
- Macro expansions cannot introduce out-of-scope identifiers or bypass capability limits.

### 3.3 Merkle AST Digests & Incremental Caching
- Compute cryptographic Merkle digests over normalized syntactic subtrees.
- Incremental compilation skips re-parsing unchanged subtrees via the Merkle cache.
- Linear zero-copy memory layout enables fast disk persistence and queries (`ooda outline`).

## 4. Type System Specifications

### 4.1 Algebraic Data Types & Exhaustive Matching
- Sum Types: Tagged unions with payload parameters (`type Shape = Circle(Float) | Rect(Float, Float);`).
- Product Types: Fixed-layout record structures (`type User = struct { id: u64, name: String };`).
- Core ADTs: Standard library primitives `Option[T]` and `Result[T, E]`.
- Pattern Matching: `match` expressions must exhaustively handle all variants.

### 4.2 Static Capability Verification (14 Tokens)
- 14 unforgeable tokens: `FsReadCap`, `FsWriteCap`, `NetCap`, `TcpCap`, `UdpCap`, `SysCap`, `ProcessCap`, `EnvCap`, `TimeCap`, `RandCap`, `AllocCap`, `ThreadCap`, `GpuCap`, `FfiCap`.
- Functions accept borrowed capability references (`&FsReadCap`) without taking ownership.
- Capability attenuation allows token holders to derive strictly narrowed subsets.
- Calling privileged functions without valid capability tokens fails compilation (`E_CAP`).

### 4.3 Typestate Protocol Verification
- Types parameterize abstract lifecycle states (`File[Unopened]`, `File[Opened]`, `File[Closed]`).
- Methods consume the input typestate and return the successor typestate to prevent invalid sequences.

### 4.4 Refinement Types & Newtypes
- Refinement Types: Base types qualified by logical predicate expressions (`type Port = Int where val >= 1 && val <= 65535;`).
- Newtypes: Zero-cost type abstractions that provide distinct identities to primitive values.

### 4.5 Static Information Flow Control (IFC) & SECRET Taint
- `SECRET:` annotations tag sensitive data fields at the type level.
- Taint analysis tracks dataflow through assignments, struct packing, and function calls.
- Compiler prevents tainted values from reaching unsealed sinks (`process_exec`, unencrypted I/O).

### 4.6 Boundary Physics & Decidability Limits
- Rice's Decidability Bound: Restrict contracts to decidable SMT logics (QF_LIA, QF_BV) with deterministic timeouts.
- CAP Monotonic Leases: Prioritize safety and consistency over availability during network partitions.
- Landauer Minimal Entropy: Structure memory in contiguous linear arenas with O(1) bulk resets.

## 5. Compiler Backends & Execution Targets

### 5.1 Backend Hierarchy
- Direct ELF64 Emission: Pure `.oo` backend emitting standalone ELF64 and WASM binaries directly.
- C Code Emission: Generates portable C code linking the oodar runtime (sourced from `openOODA/oodar/`, v2.0.0 6-subdir layout; the canonical single-TU build is `gcc oodar.c`); compiles with `gcc` or `clang`. The 18 public symbols that gained capability parameters in v2.0.0 (oo_alloc/free/write_int/read_int/checkpoint/rollback and the 12 oo_weak_*/oo_control_block_* mutators) must be emitted with the correct cap.
- LLVM IR Generation: Direct LLVM IR generation for optimization passes and SIMD vectorization.
- WebAssembly Target: Compiles to sandboxed WASM for browser environments with zero ambient authority.
- Bare-Metal Target: Minimal runtime profile for embedded architectures (x86_64, RISC-V).

### 5.2 Build Determinism & Data Layouts
- Bit-identical compilation produces reproducible binaries with normalized paths and sorted symbols.
- Support for Struct-of-Arrays (SoA) layout enables high-throughput vector processing.

## 6. Architecture Policies & Guidelines
- Builds produce bit-identical binaries by default.
- Hot paths use linear arena allocators with bulk resets; long-lived objects use deterministic ARC.
- External C function calls require `FfiCap` tokens and run inside isolated wrapper boundaries.
- Multi-agent development uses collaborative git and disk synchronization protocols.

